Recursion : When a function calls itself until a specified condition is met.
==========

In your code:

static void print() {
    System.out.println("1");
    print();
}

Every time print() is called, a new stack frame is added to the Call Stack:

print()
print()
print()
print()
main()

Since there is no base condition, print() keeps calling itself, old calls remain in the stack, and the stack eventually becomes full.

No Base Condition
        ↓
Infinite Recursive Calls
        ↓
Stack Becomes Full
        ↓
StackOverflowError

Definition: StackOverflowError occurs when the Call Stack becomes full due to too many active method calls.


Base condition is the condition that stops recursion.

Example:

if (n == 0) {
    return;
}

Without a base condition, the function keeps calling itself and may cause StackOverflowError.

In short:

Base condition tells the recursive function when to stop calling itself.

1) Sum of first N numbers
   =======================

parameterised way : 
=================

public class practice{
    static void f(int i,int sum){
        if(i<1){
            System.out.println(sum);
            return;
        }
        f(i-1,sum+i);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 3;
        f(n,0);
    }
}

explanation : This is Parameterized Recursion because we pass the calculated sum as a parameter in every recursive call.

f(3, 0)
   ↓
f(2, 3)
   ↓
f(1, 5)
   ↓
f(0, 6)
   ↓
Print 6

Here:

i → decreases toward the base condition.
sum → stores the updated sum.
i < 1 → base condition.

In short: In parameterized recursion, we carry the answer (sum) as a parameter and print it when the base condition is reached.

functional recursion:
====================

public class practice {
    static int f(int n)
    {
        if(n==0) return 0;
        return n + f(n-1);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 4;
        System.out.println(f(n));
    }
}

Functional Recursion is a type of recursion where a recursive function returns a value, and the returned value is used to calculate the final answer.

2) Reverse an array
   =================

code: public class practice{
    static void f(int l, int r, int[] arr)
    {
        if(l>=r) return;
        int temp = arr[l];
        arr[l]=arr[r];
        arr[r]=temp;
        f(l+1,r-1,arr);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1,2,3,4,5};
        int l = 0, r=arr.length-1;
        f(l,r,arr);
        for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++)
        {
            System.out.print(arr[i]+" ");
        }
    }
}

using single variable: 


public class practice{
    static void f(int i , int[] arr,int n)
    {
        if(i>=n/2) return;
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i]=arr[n-i-1];
        arr[n-i-1]=temp;
        f(i+1,arr,n);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1,2,3,4,5};
        f(0,arr,arr.length);
        for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++){
            System.out.print(arr[i]+" ");
        }
    }
}

3) Check if a string is palindrome
   ==============================

   public class practice{
    static boolean f(int i, String s,int n)
    {
        if(i>=n/2){
            return true;
        }
        if(s.charAt(i)!=s.charAt(n-i-1)){
            return false;
        }
        return f(i+1,s,n);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "madam";
        System.out.println(f(0,s,s.length()));
    }
}

return f(i + 1, s); means whatever the recursive call returns (true or false), the current function returns the same value to its caller.

Multiple Recursion Calls
=========================

q) nth Fibonacci number

code: public class practice{
    static int f(int n)
    {
        if(n<=1) return n;
        int last = f(n-1);
        int slast = f(n-2);
        return last+slast;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(f(2));
    }
}

T.C. -> 2^n (exponential)

Print all Subsequences
=======================

                        A contiguous or non-contiguous sequence, which follows the order.


code: import java.util.*;
public class practice{
    static void print(int ind,int[] arr,ArrayList<Integer> list,int n)
    {
        if(ind>=n){
            System.out.println(list);
            return;
        }
        list.add(arr[ind]);
        print(ind+1,arr,list,n);//take
        list.removeLast();
        print(ind+1,arr,list,n);//not take
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList();
        int[] arr = {3,1,2};
        int n = arr.length;
        print(0,arr,list,n);
    }
}                        

